  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1207 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nArguably the most \"interesting\" weather facet of the work week looks \nto occur Wednesday when NW flow aloft will usher in a weak cold \nfront. Winds will get slightly breezier out of the north at 10-20 \nmph, but temperatures will only be reduced by about 5-10 degrees \n(both daytime and overnight). Our dry air mass should remain largely \nunchanged behind this FROPA, with no chances for rain. Thursday \nlooks to hold more of the same, with winds becoming a touch lighter \nand temperatures a touch warmer. Ridging between deep troughs to our \neast and west will set up over the Plains on Friday, after an even \nweaker front passes through late Thursday. All in all, weather will \nbe nice and quiet throughout the work week. Early to mid November is \noften one of the most consistently pleasant stretches of \nPanhandle weather from year to year, and 2026 appears to be well \nwithin that trend. \n\nWhile there is still some signal for rain chances to potentially \nreturn this weekend, that signal remains weak. Global ensembles\nshow an upper level low progressing across the southern CONUS this\nweekend, eventually opening up and tracking across Texas. Current\nprojections favor the best moisture remaining south of our CWA, \nbut there are several members that do bring rainfall to the far \nsouth-southeast reaches of the TX Panhandle by Sunday. This only \npresents approximately 15-20% rain chances for these areas, but is\nworth monitoring. \n\nHarrel",
